---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import { getRelativeLocaleUrl } from "astro:i18n";
import config from "@/config";

const locale = Astro.currentLocale ?? config.site.lang;
---

<Layout
  title={`Money & Consumer Playbooks | ${config.site.title}`}
  description="Practical guides to reading pay stubs, understanding credit-card rules for young adults, and reviewing bank and subscription charges."
>
  <Header />
  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-4 py-9 sm:py-14">
    <header class="mb-8 max-w-3xl sm:mb-10">
      <a
        href={getRelativeLocaleUrl(locale, "reference")}
        class="text-forest decoration-manila text-sm font-semibold underline underline-offset-4"
      >
        ← Reference Library
      </a>
      <p class="text-forest mt-6 text-xs font-bold tracking-[0.16em] uppercase">
        Reference guide 04
      </p>
      <h1 class="font-heading text-forest mt-2 text-4xl font-bold sm:text-5xl">
        Money &amp; Consumer Playbooks
      </h1>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed">
        Three checklists for documents and charges young adults commonly
        encounter. Keep the original paperwork and check the rules that apply to
        your situation.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <article class="border-border bg-muted rounded-2xl border p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-forest text-xs font-bold tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">
          Playbook 01 · Work
        </p>
        <h2 class="font-heading text-forest mt-3 text-2xl font-bold">
          Reading a Pay Stub &amp; Tax Withholding
        </h2>
        <ol class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">1. Match the period.</span>
            Check the start/end dates and compare the hours to your own schedule.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">2. Compare gross to net.</span>
            Gross pay is before deductions; take-home pay is after taxes and other
            listed amounts.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">3. Review deductions.</span>
            Look for the amount and purpose of each deduction. Virginia law requires
            most employers to provide a pay statement with specified earnings information.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold"
              >4. Treat withholding as an estimate.</span
            >
            Federal and Virginia withholding are prepayments, not the final tax bill.
            A W-2 and tax return reconcile wages and taxes for the year.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <a
          href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title40.1/chapter3/section40.1-29/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
          class="text-forest decoration-manila mt-5 inline-block text-sm font-bold underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4"
        >
          Va. Code § 40.1-29 ↗
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="border-border bg-muted rounded-2xl border p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-forest text-xs font-bold tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">
          Playbook 02 · Credit
        </p>
        <h2 class="font-heading text-forest mt-3 text-2xl font-bold">
          Credit Cards &amp; the Federal CARD Act
        </h2>
        <ol class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold"
              >1. If under 21, check approval rules.</span
            >
            Issuers generally need evidence of an independent ability to make required
            payments or a qualified co-signer age 21 or older.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">2. Read every fee term.</span>
            Review APRs, annual fees, late fees, and how the issuer calculates a minimum
            payment.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">3. Know the fee standard.</span>
            Federal law requires certain penalty fees to be reasonable and proportional;
            it is not one permanent dollar cap for every charge.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">4. Keep records.</span>
            Save the application terms, statements, notices, and any communications
            with the issuer.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <a
          href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1637"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
          class="text-forest decoration-manila mt-5 inline-block text-sm font-bold underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4"
        >
          Read 15 U.S.C. § 1637 ↗
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="border-border bg-muted rounded-2xl border p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-forest text-xs font-bold tracking-[0.14em] uppercase">
          Playbook 03 · Banking
        </p>
        <h2 class="font-heading text-forest mt-3 text-2xl font-bold">
          Bank Overdrafts, Subscriptions &amp; CFPB/FTC Protections
        </h2>
        <ol class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold"
              >1. Identify the transaction.</span
            >
            Note the date, merchant, amount, and whether it was an ATM, one-time debit,
            recurring payment, or check.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">2. Check overdraft opt-in.</span
            >
            Regulation E generally requires affirmative opt-in before overdraft fees
            on covered ATM and one-time debit-card transactions—not every type of
            payment.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold"
              >3. Find subscription terms.</span
            >
            Save the sign-up screen, renewal date, total price, cancellation steps,
            and confirmation. Rules differ by service and transaction.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-forest font-bold">4. Raise a clear dispute.</span>
            Contact the provider or bank with the records. CFPB and FTC protections
            cover different products and conduct; the right agency depends on the
            issue.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-2 text-sm font-bold">
          <a
            href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/complaint/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
            class="text-forest decoration-manila underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4"
          >
            CFPB complaint ↗
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
            class="text-forest decoration-manila underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4"
          >
            FTC report ↗
          </a>
        </div>
        <a
          href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1005/17/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
          class="text-forest decoration-manila mt-4 inline-block text-sm font-bold underline decoration-2 underline-offset-4"
        >
          Regulation E § 1005.17 ↗
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-5 max-w-4xl text-xs leading-relaxed">
      These playbooks are educational and do not replace account terms,
      personalized tax guidance, or legal advice. Keep sensitive numbers and
      account details private when seeking help.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
